                  │  ENGLISH TEXT  │ │ TEXT-TO-SPEECH │
                  └────────────────┘ └────────────────┘
```

---

# 8. Exact AI / ML Logic

## Stage 1 — Video Capture

The browser opens the user's camera and obtains a live video stream.

```text
Camera
  ↓
Frame 1
Frame 2
Frame 3
...
```

The system does not necessarily need to transmit every raw frame unchanged. Depending on the implementation, feature extraction can happen near the client, at the backend, or at an optimized inference service.

---

## Stage 2 — Landmark Extraction

The vision layer extracts structured points from the frame.

### Hand

A hand is represented by a set of 21 landmarks.

Conceptually:

```text
Wrist
  ↓
Thumb joints + tip
Index joints + tip
Middle joints + tip
Ring joints + tip
Pinky joints + tip
```

### Body

Selected body/pose landmarks describe:

- shoulders,
- elbows,
- wrists,
- upper-body motion,
- other useful reference points.

### Face

Selected facial points can provide cues related to:

- mouth shape,
- eyebrow movement,
- facial configuration.

The project can start with a reduced facial feature set to keep the feature vector manageable.

---

# 9. Feature Engineering

Raw camera coordinates are not ideal because they depend on:

- signer position,
- camera distance,
- hand position in the frame,
- body location.

Therefore, the system normalizes landmarks.

A simplified reference-based transformation is:

```text
x' = x - reference_x
y' = y - reference_y
z' = z - reference_z
```

The coordinates can then be scaled using a hand/body reference distance.

The objective is to make the features describe **relative structure and movement** instead of absolute screen position.

---

# 10. Frame Feature Vector

A project configuration can combine:

```text
Left hand     → 21 landmarks
Right hand    → 21 landmarks
Pose          → selected landmarks
Face          → selected landmarks
```

For each point:

```text
x, y, z
```

The exact feature dimensionality is configuration-dependent and should be documented in the experiment logs.

Example conceptual representation:

```text
Frame t
[
  x1, y1, z1,
  x2, y2, z2,
  ...
  xn, yn, zn
]
```

---

# 11. Temporal Sequence Construction

A single frame is not enough to model movement.

The system therefore stores a rolling sequence:

```text
Frame 1
Frame 2
Frame 3
...
Frame 48
```

This becomes:

```text
Sequence = [F1, F2, ..., F48]
```

where each frame is a feature vector.

Conceptually:

```text
48 frames × feature_dimension
```

This sequence becomes the input to the temporal model.

---

# 12. Why a Temporal Model?

Consider two signs that have similar hand configurations.

A single frame may look almost identical.

However:

```text
Frame 1 → hand position
Frame 2 → movement
Frame 3 → direction
Frame 4 → final position
```

can distinguish the signs.

Therefore:

```text
Static model:
Image → sign

SignFlow AI:
Sequence → sign sequence
```

This is the core reason the project uses temporal deep learning.

---

# 13. Model Strategy

The project uses an experimental progression rather than assuming one architecture is automatically best.

## Baseline — BiLSTM

```text
Feature Sequence
      ↓
Linear Projection
      ↓
BiLSTM
      ↓
Dropout
      ↓
Decoder
      ↓
Gloss prediction
```

The BiLSTM is useful as an understandable baseline for sequence modeling.

## Main Candidate — Transformer

```text
Feature Sequence
      ↓
Linear Projection
      ↓
Positional Encoding
      ↓
Transformer Encoder
      ↓
Temporal representation
      ↓
Gloss Decoder
```

The final architecture should be selected using actual validation results.

---

# 14. Gloss Representation

Instead of immediately generating English, the vision model predicts an intermediate representation.

Example:

```text
Visual sequence
       ↓
ME NAME ARJUN
```

This is useful because it creates a clean boundary:

```text
Vision model
      ↓
Gloss
      ↓
Language layer
      ↓
English
```

This makes debugging much easier.

---

# 15. Gloss Decoder

The decoder may use:

- confidence filtering,
- blank/no-sign handling,
- duplicate suppression,
- temporal segmentation,
- sequence decoding.

A simplified example:

```text
Raw predictions:

ME
ME
ME
NAME
NAME
NAME
ARJUN
ARJUN
```

After temporal decoding:

```text
ME NAME ARJUN
```

---

# 16. Continuous Sentence Assembly

A continuous session can generate a token stream:

```text
ME
NAME
ARJUN
```

The application maintains a sentence buffer:

```text
recognized_tokens = [
    "ME",
    "NAME",
    "ARJUN"
]
```

The language layer then processes that sequence.

---

# 17. Gloss → English Layer

The second language stage converts a structured sign/gloss sequence into natural English.

Example:

```text
ME NAME ARJUN
      ↓
My name is Arjun.
```

Another example:

```text
I WANT GO COLLEGE
      ↓
I want to go to college.
```

This layer can begin as a controlled normalization system and later evolve toward a sequence-to-sequence or language-model-based approach.

Keeping it separate prevents the visual model from having to solve both sign recognition and English grammar simultaneously.

---

# 18. Text-to-Speech

Final text:

```text
My name is Arjun.
```

Optional output:

```text
🔊 My name is Arjun.
```

The speech component should be treated as an output layer, not part of the visual recognition model.

---

# 19. Real-Time Prediction Flow

```text
START
  ↓
Open Camera
  ↓
Capture Frame
  ↓
Extract Landmarks
  ↓
Normalize Features
  ↓
Append to Sequence Buffer
  ↓
Buffer Ready?
 ┌───────────────┐
 │ NO            │ → Capture next frame
 └───────────────┘
        │ YES
        ▼
Temporal Model
        ↓
Prediction Scores
        ↓
Confidence Check
        ↓
Temporal Stabilization
        ↓
Gloss Decoder
        ↓
Sentence Buffer
        ↓
English Layer
        ↓
Update UI
        ↓
Optional Speech
        ↓
Next Window
```

---

# 20. Real-Time Stabilization

Live predictions can fluctuate:

```text
GO
GO
COME
GO
GO
```

A smoothing layer can aggregate recent outputs.

Example:

```text
Recent predictions:
GO GO GO GO COME

Accepted token:
GO
```

The exact threshold, window length, and stride should be experimentally tuned.

---

