---

# 40. Privacy

The application uses a camera, so privacy should be a first-class design requirement.

Recommended principles:

- Do not store raw video by default.
- Explain what data is processed.
- Ask for consent before collecting user recordings.
- Remove identifying metadata where practical.
- Do not publish private recordings.
- Keep local processing local when feasible.
- Provide a clear reset/clear operation.
- Follow the license and usage requirements of every external dataset.
- Never represent the system as an authoritative interpreter.

---

# 41. Limitations

The system can fail because of:

- unseen signs,
- regional variations,
- signer differences,
- poor lighting,
- hand occlusion,
- body occlusion,
- camera placement,
- low frame rate,
- background clutter,
- ambiguous gestures,
- facial cues that are not captured,
- model bias from limited training data.

The English language layer can also introduce grammatical changes or errors.

Therefore:

> **System outputs should be treated as predictions, not guaranteed translations.**

